{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PbM15yMdDN7U11FxcMmVVB","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Voyager 2 Uranus closest approach January 24 1986 distance kilometers miles\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Voyager Mission Description - Ring-Moon Systems Node Home\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_2\"},{\"title\":\"Voyager 2 met Uranus 40 years ago\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Uranus Science Summary\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 1986 - Voyager 2 Flyby of Miranda - NASA\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Years Ago: Voyager 2 Explores Uranus - NASA\",\"url\":\"<redacted> of Uranus\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploration_of_Uranus\"},{\"title\":\"List of missions to the outer planets\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_missions_to_the_outer_planets\"},{\"title\":\"Voyager at Uranus - NASA Science\",\"url\":\"<redacted> (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miranda_(moon)\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here is the information about Voyager 2's closest approach to Uranus:\n\nVoyager 2's closest approach to Uranus was on January 24, 1986 at 17:59 UTC at a distance of 107,000 km from the center of Uranus. However, regarding the distance to Uranus's cloud tops specifically, the craft came within 50,640 miles (81,500 km) of its icy blue-green cloud tops.\n\nThe slight difference in these measurements is because the first figure (107,000 km) measures from the planet's center, while the second measurement (81,500 km) is from the cloud tops at the planet's surface.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59d607c1-61de-428b-926a-45965bec69c6","uuid":"e380371d-3e89-4f84-a298-0f479d95102e","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:00:06.041Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Voyager 2 Uranus closest approach January 24 1986 distance kilometers miles","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Js1BtNzynfCs2AYnDA8zcX","content":[{"title":"Voyager Mission Description - Ring-Moon Systems Node Home","url":"<redacted> 2","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_2"},{"title":"Voyager 2 met Uranus 40 years ago","url":"<redacted> Uranus Science Summary","url":"<redacted> 1986 - Voyager 2 Flyby of Miranda - NASA","url":"<redacted> Years Ago: Voyager 2 Explores Uranus - NASA","url":"<redacted> of Uranus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploration_of_Uranus"},{"title":"List of missions to the outer planets","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_missions_to_the_outer_planets"},{"title":"Voyager at Uranus - NASA Science","url":"<redacted> (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miranda_(moon)"}]},"Based on the search results, here is the information about Voyager 2's closest approach to Uranus:\n\nVoyager 2's closest approach to Uranus was on January 24, 1986 at 17:59 UTC at a distance of 107,000 km from the center of Uranus. However, regarding the distance to Uranus's cloud tops specifically, the craft came within 50,640 miles (81,500 km) of its icy blue-green cloud tops.\n\nThe slight difference in these measurements is because the first figure (107,000 km) measures from the planet's center, while the second measurement (81,500 km) is from the cloud tops at the planet's surface."],"durationSeconds":4.091304520000001,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PF4Z5pofEjpX6BH9AqaLzg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Voyager 2 Uranus 10 new moons names Shakespeare Pope discovered 1986\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"New moons of Uranus discovered by Voyager 2 | The Planetary Society\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2 met Uranus 40 years ago\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Rings and Two Moons - NASA Science\",\"url\":\"<redacted> (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ophelia_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Puck (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puck_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Voyager Uranus Science Summary\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Old Data From NASA’s Voyager 2 Solves Several Uranus Mysteries | NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL)\",\"url\":\"<redacted> at Uranus - NASA Science\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2 discovered ten moons when it flew past Uranus in 1986. Nearly 40 years later, James Webb found one it missed: Uranus's 29th known moon, a tiny world only about 10 kilometres wide hiding among the planet's inner moons.\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Searches\",\"url\":\"<redacted> me search for more specific information about the naming conventions for the Uranus moons discovered by Voyager 2, particularly regarding Shakespeare and Pope.\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Moons of Uranus | BBC Sky at Night Magazine\",\"url\":\"<redacted> the moons of Uranus are named after characters in Shakespeare - The World from PRX\",\"url\":\"<redacted> (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miranda_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Oberon (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberon_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Juliet (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juliet_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Ariel (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ariel_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Titania (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titania_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Uranus' moons: how Shakespeare influenced astronomy\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Shakespearean Moons of Uranus | Folger Shakespeare Library\",\"url\":\"<redacted> on the search results, here's what I found about Voyager 2's discoveries at Uranus in 1986:\n\n## Number of Moons Discovered\n\nBefore Voyager 2, astronomers knew five large moons: Miranda, Ariel, Umbriel, Titania and Oberon. When Voyager 2 discovered a further 10 much smaller moons orbiting Uranus at a much closer distance than the 5 major ones, these too were given Shakespearean names.\n\n## Naming Conventions: Shakespeare and Pope\n\nUranus's moons are named after characters from the works of William Shakespeare or Alexander Pope's The Rape of the Lock. When Voyager 2 discovered a further 10 much smaller moons orbiting Uranus at a much closer distance than the 5 major ones, these too were given Shakespearean names.\n\nHowever, there were exceptions: Belinda and Umbriel are both named after characters from a poem by Alexander Pope (1688-1744), and Ariel appears in works by both Shakespeare AND Pope.\n\n## Some Examples of the New Moon Names\n\nAmong the moons discovered by Voyager 2 in 1986, members started with Puck, from \"A Midsummer Night's Dream.\" Other discovered moons included Portia (1986U1), Cressida (1986 U3), and Ophelia, discovered by Richard J. Terrile / Voyager 2 on January 20, 1986.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59d607c1-61de-428b-926a-45965bec69c6","uuid":"86b45187-b7a5-44a0-a067-f91c47b789b0","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:00:11.879Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Voyager 2 Uranus 10 new moons names Shakespeare Pope discovered 1986","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01XhZ5KM9Py6J4AkozihHB3S","content":[{"title":"New moons of Uranus discovered by Voyager 2 | The Planetary Society","url":"<redacted> 2 met Uranus 40 years ago","url":"<redacted> Rings and Two Moons - NASA Science","url":"<redacted> (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ophelia_(moon)"},{"title":"Puck (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puck_(moon)"},{"title":"Voyager Uranus Science Summary","url":"<redacted> Old Data From NASA’s Voyager 2 Solves Several Uranus Mysteries | NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL)","url":"<redacted> at Uranus - NASA Science","url":"<redacted> 2 discovered ten moons when it flew past Uranus in 1986. Nearly 40 years later, James Webb found one it missed: Uranus's 29th known moon, a tiny world only about 10 kilometres wide hiding among the planet's inner moons.","url":"<redacted> Searches","url":"<redacted> me search for more specific information about the naming conventions for the Uranus moons discovered by Voyager 2, particularly regarding Shakespeare and Pope.",{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0187jsqhXUScV2i38fKm5G2i","content":[{"title":"Moons of Uranus | BBC Sky at Night Magazine","url":"<redacted> the moons of Uranus are named after characters in Shakespeare - The World from PRX","url":"<redacted> (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miranda_(moon)"},{"title":"Oberon (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberon_(moon)"},{"title":"Juliet (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juliet_(moon)"},{"title":"Ariel (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ariel_(moon)"},{"title":"Titania (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titania_(moon)"},{"title":"Uranus' moons: how Shakespeare influenced astronomy","url":"<redacted> Shakespearean Moons of Uranus | Folger Shakespeare Library","url":"<redacted> on the search results, here's what I found about Voyager 2's discoveries at Uranus in 1986:\n\n## Number of Moons Discovered\n\nBefore Voyager 2, astronomers knew five large moons: Miranda, Ariel, Umbriel, Titania and Oberon. When Voyager 2 discovered a further 10 much smaller moons orbiting Uranus at a much closer distance than the 5 major ones, these too were given Shakespearean names.\n\n## Naming Conventions: Shakespeare and Pope\n\nUranus's moons are named after characters from the works of William Shakespeare or Alexander Pope's The Rape of the Lock. When Voyager 2 discovered a further 10 much smaller moons orbiting Uranus at a much closer distance than the 5 major ones, these too were given Shakespearean names.\n\nHowever, there were exceptions: Belinda and Umbriel are both named after characters from a poem by Alexander Pope (1688-1744), and Ariel appears in works by both Shakespeare AND Pope.\n\n## Some Examples of the New Moon Names\n\nAmong the moons discovered by Voyager 2 in 1986, members started with Puck, from \"A Midsummer Night's Dream.\" Other discovered moons included Portia (1986U1), Cressida (1986 U3), and Ophelia, discovered by Richard J. Terrile / Voyager 2 on January 20, 1986."],"durationSeconds":9.419848501,"searchCount":2}}
