{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nsio7zqZUkVUzgSr6EndNr","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Voyager 2 Miranda flyby closest approach distance kilometers miles 1986\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Miranda (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miranda_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Voyager 2\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_2\"},{\"title\":\"Fact Sheet - Voyager - NASA\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2 met Uranus 40 years ago\",\"url\":\"<redacted> of Uranus\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploration_of_Uranus\"},{\"title\":\"Voyager Uranus Science Summary\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Mission Description - Ring-Moon Systems Node Home\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2's Miranda image catalog | The Planetary Society\",\"url\":\"<redacted> first Uranus flyby was performed by Voyager 2 on January 24, 1986 - Our Planet\",\"url\":\"<redacted> | December 14, 2012\",\"url\":\"<redacted> on the search results, here is the information about Voyager 2's closest approach to Miranda during its 1986 flyby:\n\nVoyager 2 passed within 26,000 kilometers of Miranda during its January 24, 1986 flyby. In miles, this is approximately 16,155 miles.\n\nThe search results also note that Miranda had a closest approach distance of 29,000 km according to NASA's Ring-Moon Systems Node data, which is slightly different from the 26,000 km figure cited elsewhere. The slight variation may reflect different measurement points or sources.\n\nIt's worth noting that while Voyager 2 made its closest approach to Uranus itself at 81,500 km (50,600 miles) on January 24, 1986, its closest approach to the individual moon Miranda was much closer at approximately 26,000-29,000 kilometers.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59d607c1-61de-428b-926a-45965bec69c6","uuid":"13888179-422c-466e-9777-90add66f726b","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:00:19.966Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Voyager 2 Miranda flyby closest approach distance kilometers miles 1986","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01PSvCn5Jj5vbLombnni7xJp","content":[{"title":"Miranda (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miranda_(moon)"},{"title":"Voyager 2","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_2"},{"title":"Fact Sheet - Voyager - NASA","url":"<redacted> 2 met Uranus 40 years ago","url":"<redacted> of Uranus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploration_of_Uranus"},{"title":"Voyager Uranus Science Summary","url":"<redacted> Mission Description - Ring-Moon Systems Node Home","url":"<redacted> 2's Miranda image catalog | The Planetary Society","url":"<redacted> first Uranus flyby was performed by Voyager 2 on January 24, 1986 - Our Planet","url":"<redacted> | December 14, 2012","url":"<redacted> on the search results, here is the information about Voyager 2's closest approach to Miranda during its 1986 flyby:\n\nVoyager 2 passed within 26,000 kilometers of Miranda during its January 24, 1986 flyby. In miles, this is approximately 16,155 miles.\n\nThe search results also note that Miranda had a closest approach distance of 29,000 km according to NASA's Ring-Moon Systems Node data, which is slightly different from the 26,000 km figure cited elsewhere. The slight variation may reflect different measurement points or sources.\n\nIt's worth noting that while Voyager 2 made its closest approach to Uranus itself at 81,500 km (50,600 miles) on January 24, 1986, its closest approach to the individual moon Miranda was much closer at approximately 26,000-29,000 kilometers."],"durationSeconds":4.605159546000003,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BBN9jZPCkhVmn3vQ92KLDP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Challenger disaster date January 28 1986\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Space Shuttle Challenger disaster\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Shuttle_Challenger_disaster\"},{\"title\":\"Today in History January 28 | Challenger Disaster & Legacy | Britannica\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Explosion: Date, Crew & Shuttle Program | HISTORY\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Accident | History | Research Starters | EBSCOhost\",\"url\":\"<redacted> space shuttle Challenger explodes after liftoff | January 28, 1986 | HISTORY\",\"url\":\"<redacted> disaster | Summary, Date, Cause, & Facts | Britannica\",\"url\":\"<redacted> STS-51L Accident - NASA\",\"url\":\"<redacted> the Challenger Seven | National Air and Space Museum\",\"url\":\"<redacted> to the Nation on the Explosion of the Space Shuttle Challenger | Ronald Reagan\",\"url\":\"<redacted> completed the web search for information about the Challenger disaster. Here's what the search results confirm:\n\nOn January 28, 1986, Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart 73 seconds into its flight, killing all seven crew members. The spacecraft disintegrated about 46,000 feet (14 km) above the Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Cape Canaveral, Florida, at 16:39:13 UTC (11:39:13 a.m. EST).\n\nIt was later determined that two rubber O-rings, which had been designed to seal sections of the rocket booster, had failed due to cold temperatures on the morning of the launch. The disaster claimed the lives of all seven crew members aboard, including Christa McAuliffe, a teacher who would have been the first civilian in space.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59d607c1-61de-428b-926a-45965bec69c6","uuid":"4283d381-33c6-4216-a48c-ac6baeaa01eb","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:00:21.198Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Challenger disaster date January 28 1986","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Du8ipWzyvirLy7gKnUzZiJ","content":[{"title":"Space Shuttle Challenger disaster","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Shuttle_Challenger_disaster"},{"title":"Today in History January 28 | Challenger Disaster & Legacy | Britannica","url":"<redacted> Explosion: Date, Crew & Shuttle Program | HISTORY","url":"<redacted> Accident | History | Research Starters | EBSCOhost","url":"<redacted> space shuttle Challenger explodes after liftoff | January 28, 1986 | HISTORY","url":"<redacted> disaster | Summary, Date, Cause, & Facts | Britannica","url":"<redacted> STS-51L Accident - NASA","url":"<redacted> the Challenger Seven | National Air and Space Museum","url":"<redacted> to the Nation on the Explosion of the Space Shuttle Challenger | Ronald Reagan","url":"<redacted> completed the web search for information about the Challenger disaster. Here's what the search results confirm:\n\nOn January 28, 1986, Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart 73 seconds into its flight, killing all seven crew members. The spacecraft disintegrated about 46,000 feet (14 km) above the Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Cape Canaveral, Florida, at 16:39:13 UTC (11:39:13 a.m. EST).\n\nIt was later determined that two rubber O-rings, which had been designed to seal sections of the rocket booster, had failed due to cold temperatures on the morning of the launch. The disaster claimed the lives of all seven crew members aboard, including Christa McAuliffe, a teacher who would have been the first civilian in space."],"durationSeconds":4.915110817,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ry4MiRZokX5CkfJMEN3KYz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Titania Oberon discovered William Herschel 1787 first moons Uranus naming tradition\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Titania | The Solar System Wiki | Fandom\",\"url\":\"<redacted> (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titania_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Jan. 11, 1787: William Herschel discovers two moons of Uranus\",\"url\":\"<redacted> | Satellite of Uranus, Icy Moon, Orbit | Britannica\",\"url\":\"<redacted> of Uranus\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moons_of_Uranus\"},{\"title\":\"Oberon (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberon_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Anniversary Post: Discovery of Titania and Oberon\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Herschel discovers Titania and Oberon, two moons of Uranus. - Today's Flashback\",\"url\":\"<redacted> on the search results, here's what I found about Titania, Oberon, and William Herschel's discoveries:\n\n## Discovery\n\nTitania was discovered by William Herschel on January 11, 1787, the same day he discovered Uranus's second largest moon, Oberon. Herschel had discovered Uranus itself six years earlier.\n\n## First Moons of Uranus\n\nWilliam Herschel discovered the first two moons, Titania and Oberon, in 1787. For nearly the next 50 years, Titania and Oberon would not be observed by any instrument other than William Herschel's.\n\n## Naming Tradition\n\nAll of Uranus's moons are named after characters created by William Shakespeare or Alexander Pope. More specifically, Titania was named by William's son, John Herschel, for a character in William Shakespeare's play A Midsummer Night's Dream. Similarly, Oberon is named after the mythical king of the fairies who appears as a character in Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59d607c1-61de-428b-926a-45965bec69c6","uuid":"01aee5e4-d3d7-403d-b834-29a67de01e3f","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:00:21.709Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Titania Oberon discovered William Herschel 1787 first moons Uranus naming tradition","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_011RTEPN2f1ySH2Wg2Z6SY9d","content":[{"title":"Titania | The Solar System Wiki | Fandom","url":"<redacted> (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titania_(moon)"},{"title":"Jan. 11, 1787: William Herschel discovers two moons of Uranus","url":"<redacted> | Satellite of Uranus, Icy Moon, Orbit | Britannica","url":"<redacted> of Uranus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moons_of_Uranus"},{"title":"Oberon (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberon_(moon)"},{"title":"Anniversary Post: Discovery of Titania and Oberon","url":"<redacted> Herschel discovers Titania and Oberon, two moons of Uranus. - Today's Flashback","url":"<redacted> on the search results, here's what I found about Titania, Oberon, and William Herschel's discoveries:\n\n## Discovery\n\nTitania was discovered by William Herschel on January 11, 1787, the same day he discovered Uranus's second largest moon, Oberon. Herschel had discovered Uranus itself six years earlier.\n\n## First Moons of Uranus\n\nWilliam Herschel discovered the first two moons, Titania and Oberon, in 1787. For nearly the next 50 years, Titania and Oberon would not be observed by any instrument other than William Herschel's.\n\n## Naming Tradition\n\nAll of Uranus's moons are named after characters created by William Shakespeare or Alexander Pope. More specifically, Titania was named by William's son, John Herschel, for a character in William Shakespeare's play A Midsummer Night's Dream. Similarly, Oberon is named after the mythical king of the fairies who appears as a character in Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream."],"durationSeconds":5.838590764,"searchCount":1}}
